--TEST--
`+` composes a carrier with its own kind only, so neither a `string` nor a `Markup` sums with a `Cli\Text` — `rule:tooling/styling-is-a-value-not-a-grammar`, `rule:core-classes/html-auto-escape`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 2 reads `Text` **+** `Text`, and the emphasis is on both sides: `+` is not
// a sink, so it has nowhere to substitute a plain string's control bytes on
// the way in. `Core\Cli\Text::plain` is where that happens, and the help says
// so rather than pointing at a member that does not exist.
var $text = Core\Cli\Text::plain("red");
echo $text + "raw", "\n";

// The two sink carriers are one rule, not one type: a `Markup` passed `rule:core-classes/html-auto-escape`
// 's rule and a `Text` passed `rule:tooling/terminal-output-is-a-sink`'s substitution, and neither
// verdict is the other's. So the cross pair is refused exactly as a `string`
// operand is, rather than composing into whichever carrier was written first.
var $markup = "<b>" as Core\Html\Markup;
echo $text + $markup, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0716]: `+` has no meaning for `Core\Cli\Text`
%A
  = help: `rule:tooling/styling-is-a-value-not-a-grammar` composes `Cli\Text` with `Cli\Text` and nothing else: lift the other operand with `Core\Cli\Text::plain(...)`, which substitutes its control bytes on the way in — `+` is not a sink and will not substitute them for you

